            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Moral purity, holiness, and the forgiveness of sin versus moral corruption, sinfulness, and separation from God. Cleansing represents justification, sanctification, and the removal of guilt through Christ's atoning sacrifice.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Psalm 51:2-10\",\"Isaiah 1:16\",\"Ezekiel 36:25\",\"Mark 7:14-23\",\"1 John 1:7-9\",\"Hebrews 9:14\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Ritual or ceremonial fitness to approach God or participate in corporate worship under the Old Covenant, often distinct from internal moral standing.\",\"basis\":[\"Leviticus 11-15\",\"Numbers 19:11-22\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Covenantal boundary markers distinguishing Israel as God's chosen people (clean) from Gentile nations (unclean), with cleansing representing the inclusion of all peoples into the New Covenant.\",\"basis\":[\"Acts 10:9-28\",\"Acts 11:1-18\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Physical health, hygiene, and the prevention of disease or contamination (often an anthropological or historical interpretation of Levitical law rather than strict theological symbolism).\",\"basis\":[\"Leviticus 13\",\"Leviticus 14\",\"Deuteronomy 23:12-14\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While Old Testament usage heavily emphasizes ritual and ceremonial categories of clean and unclean, Christian interpretation overwhelmingly views these as typological symbols for moral purity, sin, and spiritual cleansing through the blood of Christ.\"}",
